\title{#{\N \times \N \times \N} is countable}
\date{2026-07-16T15:00:38Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{Once we have this idea, we can see that #{\N \times \N \times \N} must also be [countable](00AT): we can either explicitly list them by “diagonal slices” through the corner of the infinite 3D cube; or we can just observe that we can do the #{\N \times \N \to \N} bijection twice to get a bijection ##{(\N \times \N) \times \N \to \N \times \N \to
\N}.}

\put\transclude/expanded{false}
\transclude{00HW}

\p{Likewise, #{\N^4}, #{\N^5}, and any finite Cartesian product of #{\N} with itself is also countable.}
